{
  "dom_id": "step-q78561",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\, \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?",
  "worked_solution": "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$",
